        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "7ef91ee6-a2e1-5d0b-afcb-8b844710f72f",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Johannes Megapolensis\n- Organizations: Dutch Reformed Church\n- Places: New Netherland, Fort Orange, Albany, New York state, Hudson River, North America\n- Roles: dominie, pastor, Protestant missionary\n- Topics: Dutch colonization, missions to Indians\n\nFacts:\n- Fort Orange was the first permanent Dutch settlement in New Netherland, and the present-day city of Albany, New York developed at that site.\n- Johannes Megapolensis was born in 1603 and died in 1670.\n- In 1642, Johannes Megapolensis became a dominie, or pastor, of the Dutch Reformed Church in the Dutch colony of New Netherland in present-day New York state.\n- Johannes Megapolensis served for several years at Fort Orange on the upper Hudson River in present-day Albany, New York.\n- Johannes Megapolensis is credited with being the first Protestant missionary to the Indians in North America.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "dbf546bb-8faf-5072-a029-567dee84995a",
        "text": "Page: Johannes Megapolensis. Sentence: Johannes Megapolensis (1603–1670) was a \"dominie\" (pastor) of the Dutch Reformed Church in the Dutch colony of New Netherland (present-day New York state in the United States), beginning in 1642.\nPage: Johannes Megapolensis. Sentence: Serving for several years at Fort Orange (present-day Albany, New York) on the upper Hudson River, he is credited with being the first Protestant missionary to the Indians in North America.\nPage: Fort Orange (New Netherland). Sentence: Fort Orange (Dutch: \"Fort Oranje\" ) was the first permanent Dutch settlement in New Netherland; the present-day city of Albany, New York developed at this site.",
